{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1795453744,"nonce":"y1D6RjZ5fBu/kRrHXsy+WuentiQ=","message":"Split of taffy's time in a month turn, at 32008e10 (temporary timing in LayoutTree::compute_layout, removed after; cn_demo_costs, 40 turns, release). Each layout with work to do was timed three ways on clones of the TaffyTree: the full layout, the same without rounding, and only the subtrees a boundary scheme would still lay out. A boundary is found walking down from the root through dirty nodes: below a node whose dirty children all kept their size and position, carry on into them; elsewhere stop, and lay that node out alone at its size.\n\n99 layouts in 40 turns, about 2.5 a turn; the page has about 3,240 to 3,610 taffy nodes.\n\n- Insert (new month), 40 layouts: 609 us each. Rounding 16%, boundary subtrees 35% (2 or 3 boundaries, about 520 nodes), ancestors 49%.\n- The others (old month removed, the style write after it), 59 layouts: 296 us each. Rounding 25%, boundary subtrees 1% (the container, its kept page answered from cache), ancestors 74%.\n- All together: rounding 20%, boundary subtrees 20%, ancestors 60%.\n\nSo about 80% of taffy's time in a turn is work a relayout boundary and rounding only the relaid subtree would skip. Taffy is about a quarter of the structural update, so the gain is about a fifth of the structural update.","files":null}]}